is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
I cannot reproduce a network-side failure. End-to-end reachability and L2/L3 health look normal across all access VLANs. Every workstation in the lab currently has its NIC up with the correct IP and default route. Representative checks:
- Intra-VLAN: pc1↔pc2 (10.5.0.x), pc3↔pc4 (10.6.0.x), pc12↔pc13 (172.16.30.x) all succeed with 0% loss and sub‑2 ms RTTs.
- Gateway reachability: from each VLAN a host can ping its default gateway: 10.5.0.254, 10.6.0.254, 172.16.10.254, 172.16.20.254, 172.16.30.254 all respond (0% loss, ~0.4–2 ms RTT).
- Inter-VLAN: multiple pairs (e.g., dc_pc5→pc6, pc10→pc13, pc9→pc6) are reachable with 0% loss.

Mechanistically, if a switchport or VLAN were misconfigured/err-disabled, we would expect one host’s eth0 to be DOWN or ARP/gateway pings to fail on that subnet; none of those signatures appear. Localization: all tested access segments on switch1–switch4 are passing traffic, and all workstations’ eth0 show state UP with valid /24 addressing and default routes.

Root cause: Not reproducible at this time; the lab network elements and access ports are operational, suggesting the original issue was transient or endpoint/cabling specific.

Corrective fix: No network changes required. If the desk still sees the problem, reseat/replace the patch lead, verify the NIC link light and that the host retains the correct static IP/default gateway, and, if needed, move that endpoint to another known-good access port while checking the original port for errdisable/port-security or VLAN mismatch. Please provide the exact hostname/port if the issue recurs so we can target that interface. 
=== END ===